**** Changes from 2026.08.12 (onken)

- Cleanups in SCP pool:
  Guard the maximum worker number with the pool mutex, remove an
  ineffective assignment to a by-value parameter, and update outdated
  documentation.
  Affects: dcmnet/include/dcmtk/dcmnet/scppool.h
           dcmnet/libsrc/scppool.cc

- Fixed no-op round in SCP pool test:
  The second round of dcmnet_scp_pool iterated over the previously
  cleared SCU vector and thus tested nothing. Also check the SCU results
  only after join(), so that unfinished results cannot be read.
  Affects: dcmnet/tests/tpool.cc

- Fixed leaked worker slots in SCP pool:
  A worker whose setAssociation() or start() call failed remained in the
  busy list forever, leaking one of the pool's worker slots and making
  the pool destructor wait for eternity. Such workers are now removed
  from the busy list and deleted. Added regression test
  dcmnet_scp_pool_error_resilience.
  Affects: dcmnet/libsrc/scppool.cc
           dcmnet/tests/tests.cc
           dcmnet/tests/tpool.cc

- Keep SCP pool listening on transient errors:
  Errors while starting a worker for an incoming association (memory
  exhaustion, thread creation failure) made the listen loop exit and
  thereby permanently stopped the server. Such errors now lead to a
  refusal of the affected association only, with a warning being logged.
  Affects: dcmnet/include/dcmtk/dcmnet/scppool.h
           dcmnet/libsrc/scppool.cc

- Fixed SCP pool blocking on re-used workers:
  Pool workers are based on OFThread, whose thread terminates when run()
  returns, so "waking up" an idle worker via rerun() executed the whole
  association synchronously in the pool's listener thread: once a worker
  had been re-used, a single slow client blocked the entire server.
  A worker thread now handles exactly one association and terminates
  afterwards; the listen loop continuously joins and deletes finished
  workers, so the maximum number of concurrent associations configured
  via setMaxThreads() is honored at all times. Added regression test
  dcmnet_scp_pool_concurrency.
  Affects: dcmnet/include/dcmtk/dcmnet/scppool.h
           dcmnet/libsrc/scppool.cc
           dcmnet/tests/tests.cc
           dcmnet/tests/tpool.cc

**** Changes from 2026.08.07 (onken)

- Avoid Code copy in JPEG-LS decoder fast path:
  The k < 16 out-of-bounds guard indexed decodingTables[] via a by-value
  Code plus a throwaway Code(0, 0), adding a copy to the decoder's hottest
  loop. Use a pointer that is null for k >= 16 and keep the original
  const-reference lookup; no functional change.
  Enhances commit 4ac133.
  Affects: dcmjpls/libcharls/scan.h

**** Changes from 2026.08.06 (onken)

- Fixed unbounded allocation for a basic film box:
  The image display format of a basic film box determines how many image box
  objects are created, but the values received from the remote application were
  only checked against the layouts of the printer configuration.  That check is
  skipped when the configuration does not list any, so a single request could
  ask for an arbitrary number of image boxes and exhaust the memory of the
  print server.  The number of image boxes is now limited in any case.
  Thanks to Daniel Birtwhistle (GE HealthCare) for the report and analysis.
  This closes DCMTK issue #1262.
  Affects: dcmpstat/include/dcmtk/dcmpstat/dvpssp.h
           dcmpstat/libsrc/dvpssp.cc

- Fixed command execution after a rejected C-STORE:
  The option --exec-on-reception was evaluated whenever the DIMSE exchange
  itself had succeeded, which is also the case when the object was rejected,
  for example because it has no Study Instance UID and one of the --sort-xxx
  options is used.  No output file has been written for such a request, so the
  command was executed with the file name of an earlier one, which could even
  have been received from a different peer, or with no file name at all, which
  terminated the application.  The command is now only executed if the object
  has really been stored.
  Thanks to Daniel Birtwhistle (GE HealthCare) for the report and analysis.
  This closes DCMTK issue #1259.
  Affects: dcmnet/apps/storescp.cc

- Fixed out-of-bounds access on a full study index:
  If the study index is full and the study of an incoming image is not
  registered yet, the lookup returns the number of entries to report that.  Two
  of its callers used that value as an array index without checking it, which
  read one element past the study descriptor array, and, depending on what was
  read there, also wrote a complete record including the Study Instance UID of
  the image past the end.  Both callers now check the value first, as the third
  one has always done.
  Thanks to Daniel Birtwhistle (GE HealthCare) for the report and analysis.
  This closes DCMTK issue #1253.
  Affects: dcmqrdb/libsrc/dcmqrdbi.cc

- Fixed endless loop in the JPEG-LS codec:
  The JPEG-LS parameter RESET is read as a 16-bit value but kept in a single
  byte by the run mode context, so a value larger than 255 was truncated.  A
  value truncated to zero is never reached by the counter it is compared with,
  and the computation of the Golomb code length then looped forever, which made
  the decoder spin at full CPU load on a crafted image.  Such a value is now
  rejected, both when decoding and when encoding.
  Thanks to Daniel Birtwhistle (GE HealthCare) for the report and analysis.
  This closes DCMTK issue #1257.
  Affects: dcmjpls/libcharls/header.cc

- Fixed NULL pointer copy when parsing an N-GET-RQ:
  The attribute identifier list of an N-GET request was copied into a buffer
  without checking whether that buffer could be allocated, and without checking
  whether the value of the element could be read at all.  Since the length of
  the element is determined by the sender, a failing allocation made the copy
  dereference a NULL pointer, which crashed the application before the request
  was dispatched.  Such a list is now reported as absent instead.
  Thanks to Daniel Birtwhistle (GE HealthCare) for the report and analysis.
  This closes DCMTK issue #1260.
  Affects: dcmnet/libsrc/dimcmd.cc

- Fixed path escape when appending to a DICOMDIR:
  If a study record contained no Study Instance UID, the referenced file was
  opened in order to read the UID from there.  The referenced file ID was used
  as a path without any checking, so a crafted DICOMDIR could have any file
  read and parsed, and could steer where new records were attached.  Such a
  file ID is now rejected, and it is resolved relative to the directory of the
  DICOMDIR file instead of the current directory.
  Thanks to Daniel Birtwhistle (GE HealthCare) for the report and analysis.
  This closes DCMTK issue #1266.
  Affects: dcmdata/libsrc/dcddirif.cc

- Moved File ID check from dcmnet to dcmdata:
  The check whether a Referenced File ID read from a DICOMDIR is safe to be used
  as a relative path name is not specific to the Storage SCU, so it was moved
  from DcmStorageSCU to DicomDirInterface, where it can also be used by the
  DICOMDIR handling itself.  Also added an option to accept lowercase letters,
  which are not permitted by the standard but do occur in practice and are just
  as safe.  The associated test was moved and extended accordingly.
  Added:   dcmdata/tests/tdicdir.cc
  Affects: dcmdata/include/dcmtk/dcmdata/dcddirif.h
           dcmdata/libsrc/dcddirif.cc
           dcmdata/tests/CMakeLists.txt
           dcmdata/tests/Makefile.in
           dcmdata/tests/tests.cc
           dcmnet/include/dcmtk/dcmnet/dstorscu.h
           dcmnet/libsrc/dstorscu.cc
           dcmnet/tests/CMakeLists.txt
           dcmnet/tests/Makefile.in
           dcmnet/tests/tests.cc
  Removed: dcmnet/tests/tstorescu.cc

- Fixed uninitialized memory in RLE decoder output:
  When an RLE stripe ended prematurely, a warning was printed announcing that
  the remaining pixels would be filled, but the complete stripe was copied to
  the uncompressed pixel data instead.  Since the output buffer of the RLE
  decoder is neither initialized nor cleared between stripes, everything beyond
  the decoded data was uninitialized memory or the content of the preceding
  stripe, and ended up in the decompressed image.  The remaining pixels are now
  filled with the last decoded pixel, which is what decodeFrame() has always
  done, so that both decoding methods again yield the same result.
  Thanks to Daniel Birtwhistle (GE HealthCare) for the report and analysis.
  This closes DCMTK issue #1267.
  Affects: dcmdata/include/dcmtk/dcmdata/dcrledec.h
           dcmdata/libsrc/dcrleccd.cc

- Fixed buffer overflow in multi-frame conversion:
  All frames of an image are stored in a single PixelData element, whose length
  is a 32-bit value.  The overall size of the frames was only truncated to 32
  bits when that element was created, while the frames themselves were copied
  using the untruncated frame length and frame offsets, i.e. beyond the end of
  the allocated buffer.  Such images are now rejected.  In addition, each frame
  is now checked to fit into the buffer, so that passing a frame number larger
  than the number of frames the buffer was allocated for is refused instead of
  writing past its end.
  Thanks to Daniel Birtwhistle (GE HealthCare) for the report and analysis.
  This closes DCMTK issue #1270.
  Affects: dcmdata/include/dcmtk/dcmdata/libi2d/i2d.h
           dcmdata/libi2d/i2d.cc

- Fixed buffer overflow in document encapsulation:
  The length of an encapsulated document is stored in a 32-bit DICOM element,
  but the size of the document file was only truncated to 32 bits when the
  element was created, not when its content was written.  A document larger
  than 4 GiB thus caused a much too small buffer to be allocated, which was
  then written and read beyond its end.  Such documents are now rejected.
  Thanks to Daniel Birtwhistle (GE HealthCare) for the report and analysis.
  This closes DCMTK issue #1265.
  Affects: dcmdata/libsrc/dcencdoc.cc

- Fixed integer overflow in JPEG decompression check:
  The check whether the decompressed image fits into the frame buffer
  multiplied the row size, a size_t, by the image height, a 32-bit unsigned
  int. On platforms where size_t is 32 bits wide the product could wrap, so
  the check passed although the image was far too large, and the following
  copy wrote past the frame buffer. Compare in 64-bit arithmetic instead.
  The same applies to all three IJG precision variants.
  Thanks to Daniel Birtwhistle (GE HealthCare) for the report and analysis.
  This closes DCMTK issue #1263.
  Affects: dcmjpeg/libsrc/djdijg12.cc
           dcmjpeg/libsrc/djdijg16.cc
           dcmjpeg/libsrc/djdijg8.cc

- Fixed integer overflow in overlay plane handling:
  The expected length of the overlay data and the size of the buffer for
  the (6xxx,3000) overlay data were computed in "unsigned long", which is
  only 32 bits wide on some platforms. There, both products could wrap:
  the length check then accepted an overlay far larger than the data
  received, and the buffer allocated for it was much too small for the
  loop that fills it. Compute both values with OFStandard::safeMult() and
  safeAdd() and refuse the overlay if they are not representable.
  Thanks to Daniel Birtwhistle (GE HealthCare) for the report and analysis.
  This closes DCMTK issue #1264.
  Affects: dcmimgle/libsrc/diovpln.cc

- Fixed out-of-bounds read in planar color conversion:
  The conversion of planar ARGB, CMYK, HSV and YBR pixel data walks all
  planes of all frames without checking that the pixel data actually
  contains that many samples, so a short or partial input was read beyond
  the buffer. For CMYK the values read were also stored in the rendered
  image. The YBR check that was introduced for this purpose used a pixel
  count capped at the size of the intermediate buffer and, therefore, could
  not detect that whole planes or frames were missing. Compare the uncapped
  number of input pixels with the number of pixels of the image instead.
  Thanks to Daniel Birtwhistle (GE HealthCare) for the report and analysis.
  This closes DCMTK issue #1261.
  Affects: dcmimage/include/dcmtk/dcmimage/diargpxt.h
           dcmimage/include/dcmtk/dcmimage/dicmypxt.h
           dcmimage/include/dcmtk/dcmimage/dihsvpxt.h
           dcmimage/include/dcmtk/dcmimage/diybrpxt.h

- Fixed out-of-bounds read in monochrome rendering:
  The optimization LUT was sized from the value range of the intermediate
  pixel data, but it is indexed by the pixel value minus the truncated
  minimum. Both values are truncated to integers independently, so the LUT
  was one entry short whenever the fractional part of the maximum exceeds
  the one of the minimum, e.g. for a Modality LUT with a non-integer
  rescale slope or intercept, and the largest pixel value read one entry
  past the LUT. Derive the number of entries from the same truncated
  domain that is used for the index.
  Thanks to Daniel Birtwhistle (GE HealthCare) for the report and analysis.
  This closes DCMTK issue #1271.
  Affects: dcmimgle/include/dcmtk/dcmimgle/dimoopxt.h

- Fixed overflow in getUncompressedFrameSize():
  The overflow check only covered the multiplication with Bits Allocated,
  while the number of pixels itself (rows * cols * samplesPerPixel) was
  computed unchecked and, since the operands are 16-bit, as int. For a large
  image this overflowed and the function reported success with a truncated
  frame size; the branch for a bit depth that is not a multiple of 8 was not
  checked at all. Compute all steps with OFStandard::safeMult() and safeAdd()
  and report an error if the frame size is not representable.
  Thanks to Daniel Birtwhistle (GE HealthCare) for the report and analysis.
  This closes DCMTK issue #1269.
  Affects: dcmdata/libsrc/dcelem.cc

- Fixed uninitialized memory in DICOMDIR verify():
  DcmDicomDir::verify() sized its MRDR reference table by an upper bound
  that assumes every directory record to be an MRDR, but only filled in the
  entries for the records that actually are MRDRs. The statement adjusting
  the table size to the real value was commented out, so the remaining
  entries were used uninitialized: for a DICOMDIR with orphaned records
  this called a member function through an indeterminate pointer. Enable
  the adjustment again.
  Thanks to Daniel Birtwhistle (GE HealthCare) for the report and analysis.
  This closes DCMTK issue #1256.
  Affects: dcmdata/libsrc/dcdicdir.cc

- Fixed out-of-bounds read in DVPS IPC strings:
  Extracting a string from the message payload assigned a character pointer
  to an OFString, i.e. it relied on a terminating NUL byte, which a payload
  received from a peer does not necessarily contain. In addition, the check
  that the string lies within the received data added the string length,
  which is also taken from the payload, without detecting an overflow, so a
  large length passed the check. Compute the end offset with
  OFStandard::safeAdd() and extract at most the announced number of bytes.
  Thanks to Daniel Birtwhistle (GE HealthCare) for the report and analysis.
  This closes DCMTK issue #1272.
  Affects: dcmpstat/libsrc/dvpsmsg.cc

- Fixed buffer overflow in DVPS IPC message receive:
  The payload length was taken from the received message header and added
  to PAYLOAD_OFFSET in Uint32 arithmetic. For a large value the sum wrapped
  around, so the payload buffer was not enlarged although the subsequent
  read was performed with the full (huge) length, overrunning the buffer
  with data supplied by the peer. Reject implausible payload lengths before
  the size is computed and document the limit as
  DVPSIPCMessage::maximumPayloadSize.
  Thanks to Daniel Birtwhistle (GE HealthCare) for the report and analysis.
  This closes DCMTK issue #1268.
  Affects: dcmpstat/include/dcmtk/dcmpstat/dvpsmsg.h
           dcmpstat/libsrc/dvpsmsg.cc

- Fixed undefined behaviour in OFStandard::safeMult():
  The overflow was detected by computing the product first and dividing
  afterwards. For types narrower than "int" both operands are promoted to
  (signed) "int", so the multiplication itself could already overflow, e.g.
  65535 * 65535 for Uint16, which is undefined behaviour. Check the operands
  before multiplying instead, like safeAdd() already does. Also added test
  cases for Uint8 and Uint16, which were not covered so far.
  Affects: ofstd/include/dcmtk/ofstd/ofstd.h
           ofstd/tests/tofstd.cc

- Fixed integer overflow in pixel count computation:
  The pixel counts used to size the input pixel buffers could overflow and
  the under-sized buffers were then overrun by the conversion: the frame
  size was computed in "unsigned long" (32 bits on ILP32 and LLP64), the
  uncompressed path multiplied 8*length_B1 as 32-bit int, and the
  partial-access path explicitly truncated the element count to Uint32.
  Use OFStandard::safeMult() and safeAdd() for these computations and only
  process the image if the counts are representable on this platform;
  otherwise it is rejected instead of corrupting memory. The remaining
  limitation is documented in DiImage::convertPixelData().
  Thanks to Daniel Birtwhistle (GE HealthCare) for the report and analysis.
  This closes DCMTK issue #1255.
  Affects: dcmimgle/include/dcmtk/dcmimgle/diinpxt.h
           dcmimgle/libsrc/diimage.cc

- Fixed integer overflow in JPEG-LS encoder check:
  The JPEG-LS raw encoder verified that enough pixel data was present via
  bytesAllocated*samplesPerPixel*columns*rows*frames, but the 16-bit
  operands were multiplied as 32-bit int and wrapped for large geometries,
  so a crafted image with tiny PixelData passed the check and the encoder
  then read and wrote far past the buffer. Evaluate the product in 64-bit
  before the comparison.
  Thanks to Daniel Birtwhistle (GE HealthCare) for the report and analysis.
  This closes DCMTK issue #1254.
  Affects: dcmjpls/libsrc/djcodece.cc

- Fixed out-of-bounds write in lossless JPEG decoder:
  The lossless JPEG input controller sized a local component-buffer array
  by MAX_COMPS_IN_SCAN (4) but indexed it by the frame component index,
  which can reach MAX_COMPONENTS-1 (9). A crafted multi-scan lossless JPEG
  image thus wrote a heap pointer past the 4-element stack array. Size the
  array by MAX_COMPONENTS, matching the sibling arrays on this path.
  Thanks to Daniel Birtwhistle (GE HealthCare) for the report and analysis.
  This closes DCMTK issue #1252.
  Affects: dcmjpeg/libijg12/jddiffct.c
           dcmjpeg/libijg16/jddiffct.c
           dcmjpeg/libijg8/jddiffct.c

- Added option to accept all return keys to wlmscpfs:
  Added new command line options --enable-all-return and --disable-all-return
  (default) to wlmscpfs. If enabled, arbitrary non-sequence attributes on the
  main level of the C-FIND request are accepted as return key attributes and
  filled with the values found in the matching worklist files. Sequence
  attributes and attributes within sequences are not affected. Values are now
  retrieved from the worklist files in a generic way that also covers binary
  value representations such as US. Also added related unit tests.
  Added:   dcmwlm/tests/twlallret.cc
  Affects: dcmnet/tests/Makefile.dep
           dcmwlm/apps/wlcefs.cc
           dcmwlm/apps/wlcefs.h
           dcmwlm/docs/wlmscpfs.man
           dcmwlm/include/dcmtk/dcmwlm/wlds.h
           dcmwlm/libsrc/wlds.cc
           dcmwlm/libsrc/wldsfs.cc
           dcmwlm/libsrc/wlfsim.cc
           dcmwlm/tests/CMakeLists.txt
           dcmwlm/tests/Makefile.dep
           dcmwlm/tests/Makefile.in
           dcmwlm/tests/tests.cc

**** Changes from 2026.07.20 (onken)

- Fixed out-of-bounds read in JPEG-LS decoder:
  JlsCodec::DoRegular() (decoder) indexed the 16-entry decodingTables[]
  array with the Golomb parameter k returned by JlsContext::GetGolomb(),
  which can exceed 15 for crafted or corrupt input (the ASSERT(k <= 32)
  guard is a no-op in release builds). decodingTables[k] then read past
  the end of the array. Restrict the fast-path table lookup to k < 16 and
  fall back to the generic DecodeValue() path otherwise; the tables for
  k >= 8 are empty by construction, so valid streams are unaffected.
  Thanks to Samuel Paschuan (4nt1, xmartlab) for the report and analysis.
  This closes DCMTK issue #1250.
  Affects: dcmjpls/libcharls/scan.h

**** Changes from 2026.07.17 (onken)

- Added option to img2dcm to keep selected APPn:
  The JPEG input plugin of img2dcm removes all APPn markers from the
  encapsulated stream by default. This also drops the Adobe APP14 marker
  (0xFFEE), whose color transform flag some JPEG decoders and viewers rely
  on to decide whether a YCbCr to RGB conversion has to be applied during
  decoding. Without it such decoders may render the image with wrong
  colors.
  The existing --keep-appn option can only keep all APPn markers at once.
  The new --keep-app <number> option keeps a single APPn marker (0..15)
  and may be given multiple times, e.g. --keep-app 14 to retain the Adobe
  marker. The marker is kept by number, irrespective of its actual
  application identifier. The default behaviour (removing all APPn
  markers) is left unchanged.
  Thanks to Mathieu Malaterre for the idea.
  This closes DCMTK issue #1249.
  Affects: dcmdata/apps/img2dcm.cc
           dcmdata/docs/img2dcm.man
           dcmdata/include/dcmtk/dcmdata/libi2d/i2djpgs.h
           dcmdata/libi2d/i2djpgs.cc

**** Changes from 2026.07.17 (riesmeier)

- Introduced module-specific condition constant:
  Introduced module-specific condition constant that is used when the
  maximum nesting level is exceeded, e.g. when reading Content Items from
  an XML document. Before, a general constant from the dcmdata module was
  used, but the associated text message was not appropriate.
  Affects: dcmsr/include/dcmtk/dcmsr/dsrtypes.h
           dcmsr/libsrc/dsrdoctn.cc
           dcmsr/libsrc/dsrtypes.cc

**** Changes from 2026.07.15 (riesmeier)

- Removed unsupported Storage SOP Classes:
  Removed two Storage SOP Classes that were added to the manpage by
  mistake (the "long list" of Storage SOP Classes has been full for a
  very long time).
  Affects: dcmnet/docs/getscu.man

- Added support for three new Storage SOP Classes:
  Added definition of new Storage SOP Class UIDs introduced with Supplement
  252 (For Processing CT SOP Classes). Also added support for these Storage
  SOP Classes to the various networking tools.
  Affects: dcmdata/include/dcmtk/dcmdata/dcuid.h
           dcmdata/libsrc/dcuid.cc
           dcmnet/docs/movescu.man
           dcmnet/docs/storescp.man
           dcmnet/etc/storescp.cfg
           dcmnet/etc/storescu.cfg
           dcmqrdb/docs/dcmqrscp.man
           dcmqrdb/etc/dcmqrprf.cfg

**** Changes from 2026.07.14 (onken)

- Fixed remote memory leak in N-GET-RQ handling:
  The Attribute Identifier List (0000,1005) of an incoming N-GET-RQ is
  parsed into a heap buffer by getAttributeList() in dimcmd.cc, but the
  enclosing T_DIMSE_Message has no destructor. Neither DcmSCP nor
  DVPSPrintSCP freed this buffer after use, so every N-GET-RQ with a
  non-empty attribute list leaked a few bytes. A remote peer could
  repeat such requests to exhaust the SCP's memory. Now freed in both
  N-GET receive paths.
  Thanks to Eneshan Erdoğan Karaca for report and analysis.
  This closes DCMTK issue #1248.
  Affects: dcmnet/libsrc/scp.cc
           dcmpstat/libsrc/dvpsprt.cc

**** Changes from 2026.07.05 (onken)

- Fix int conversion warning on some compilers.
  Affects: dcmimgle/libsrc/diluptab.cc

**** Changes from 2026.07.03 (onken)

- Fixed overflow in numeric VR value parsing:
  The putString() implementations of the binary numeric value
  representations (AT, FL, FD, OB/OW, SL, SS, SV, UL, US, UV) allocated an
  array sized by the value multiplicity determined from the input string,
  without an upper bound. A very large multiplicity could therefore
  overflow the allocation size computation and lead to a heap buffer
  overflow. Each of these methods now rejects a value whose overall length
  would exceed the 32-bit DICOM value length limit before allocating.
  Thanks to Matthias Deeg (SySS GmbH) for the report and analysis.
  This closes DCMTK issue #1247.
  Affects: dcmdata/libsrc/dcvrat.cc
           dcmdata/libsrc/dcvrfd.cc
           dcmdata/libsrc/dcvrfl.cc
           dcmdata/libsrc/dcvrobow.cc
           dcmdata/libsrc/dcvrsl.cc
           dcmdata/libsrc/dcvrss.cc
           dcmdata/libsrc/dcvrsv.cc
           dcmdata/libsrc/dcvrul.cc
           dcmdata/libsrc/dcvrus.cc
           dcmdata/libsrc/dcvruv.cc

- Fixed integer overflow in RLE encoder size check:
  The RLE codec encoder validated the expected amount of Pixel Data using
  32-bit arithmetic, so crafted Rows/Columns/NumberOfFrames values could
  make the product wrap to a small value and let a too-small Pixel Data
  element pass the check. The stripe encoding loop then read past the
  Pixel Data allocation (heap out-of-bounds read). The expected size is
  now computed using 64-bit arithmetic.
  Thanks to Matthias Deeg (SySS GmbH) for the report and analysis.
  This closes DCMTK issue #1246.
  Affects: dcmdata/libsrc/dcrlecce.cc

- Fixed potential buffer overflow in xml2dcm:
  DcmXMLParseHelper measured the size of an external binary file
  (referenced via binary="file") as size_t but narrowed it to a 32-bit
  value for the buffer allocation, while the full size_t length was used
  for reading. A file larger than the 32-bit DICOM value length limit
  therefore allocated a truncated buffer and then overflowed it during
  fread(). Such files are now rejected before allocation.
  Thanks to Matthias Deeg (SySS GmbH) for the report and analysis.
  This closes DCMTK issue #1245.
  Affects: dcmdata/libdcxml/xml2dcm.cc

- Fixed path traversal when reading from DICOMDIR:
  The Referenced File ID (0004,1500) read from a DICOMDIR was used as a
  relative pathname without validation, so a crafted DICOMDIR could reference
  files outside the file-set directory (via ".." components or an absolute
  path). As a result, "dcmsend --read-from-dicomdir" could read and transmit
  such files. Referenced File IDs are now validated against the DICOM File ID
  character set before use, which rejects these traversal attempts. A unit
  test for the new DcmStorageSCU::isReferencedFileIDSafe() is included.
  Thanks to Matthias Deeg (SySS GmbH) for the report and analysis.
  This closes DCMTK issue #1244.
  Added:   dcmnet/tests/tstorescu.cc
  Affects: dcmnet/include/dcmtk/dcmnet/dstorscu.h
           dcmnet/libsrc/dstorscu.cc
           dcmnet/tests/CMakeLists.txt
           dcmnet/tests/Makefile.in
           dcmnet/tests/tests.cc

- Fixed overflow in DICOMDIR external icon loading:
  The width and height parsed from an external PGM icon file were
  multiplied using 32-bit arithmetic, which could overflow and yield an
  undersized pixel buffer that the icon scaler then read past (heap
  out-of-bounds read). Dimensions that do not fit into 16 bits (the
  scaler's limit) are now rejected, which also makes the buffer size
  computation safe.
  Thanks to Matthias Deeg (SySS GmbH) for the report and analysis.
  This closes DCMTK issue #1243.
  Affects: dcmdata/libsrc/dcddirif.cc

- Regenerated Makefile dependencies.
  Affects: dcmqrdb/tests/Makefile.dep
           oficonv/libsrc/Makefile.dep

- Fixed out-of-bounds read for 3-comp. JPEG-LS img:
  On the custom-RESET path (RESET present and not the basic default),
  JlsCodecFactory::GetCodec() always created a single-sample codec,
  ignoring the number of components and the interleave mode. For a
  3-component, sample-interleaved (ILV_SAMPLE) image the resulting
  scanline buffer was sized for a single sample, but the triplet color
  transform reads the full declared width in triplets, so decoding read
  past the end of that buffer. Select the pixel type from the component
  count and interleave mode exactly as GetCodecImpl() does, creating a
  triplet codec for 3-component ILV_SAMPLE images (and leaving no codec
  for unsupported sample-interleaved component counts, as before).
  Thanks to quellsec.dev for the report and analysis.
  This fixes DCMTK issue #1242.
  Affects: dcmjpls/libcharls/jpegls.cc

- Fixed double free of palette color LUT data:
  IODPaletteColorLUTModule::checkDataConsistency() retrieved the red,
  green and blue palette color LUT data via
  getXPaletteColorLookupTableData() - each returning a fresh new[] copy -
  and called delete[] after each. The accessors leave their out-parameter
  unchanged on failure, and the pointer was not reset between calls, so a
  palette with a valid red LUT but a malformed green (or blue) LUT freed
  the red copy twice. Reset the pointer to NULL after each delete[] so a
  subsequent failed accessor cannot trigger a second delete[] of the
  previous buffer.
  Thanks to quellsec.dev for the report and analysis.
  This fixes DCMTK issue #1241.
  Affects: dcmiod/libsrc/modpalettecolorlut.cc

- Fixed buffer overflow in overlay conversion:
  DiOverlayPlane::create6xxx3000Data() sized its output buffer for a
  single continuous bitstream of Width*Height*Frames bits (count8 bytes),
  but flushed the trailing partial byte at the end of every frame. When
  the overlay plane size (Width*Height) was not a multiple of 8, each
  frame emitted one extra byte, so a multi-frame overlay ran past the end
  of the allocation. Flush the partial byte only once, after all frames
  have been written, matching the continuous-bitstream layout the buffer
  is sized for (overlay data is a single bit stream, not byte-aligned per
  frame).
  Thanks to quellsec.dev for the report and analysis.
  This fixes DCMTK issue #1240.
  Affects: dcmimgle/libsrc/diovpln.cc

- Fixed out-of-bounds write in BE 8 bit LUT exp:
  On big-endian hosts, DiLookupTable::checkTable() expanded an
  8-bit-allocated lookup table into a freshly allocated Uint16[Count]
  buffer by looping (Count + 1) / 2 times and writing two entries per
  iteration. For an odd Count this wrote Count + 1 entries, one Uint16
  past the end of the buffer, storing an attacker-controlled byte. Loop
  over Count / 2 pairs and copy the trailing odd entry separately so that
  exactly Count entries are written, matching the allocation and the
  little-endian branch.
  Thanks to quellsec.dev for the report and analysis.
  This fixes DCMTK issue #1239.
  Affects: dcmimgle/libsrc/diluptab.cc

- Fixed out-of-bounds read in JPEG-LS parsing:
  JlsCodec::DecodeScan() took the scan-header length from the SOS marker
  and copied that many bytes out of the compressed input, checking the
  length only against the local 20-byte buffer, never against the bytes
  actually remaining in the input. A crafted JPEG-LS fragment with an
  inflated SOS length byte made the copy read past the end of the
  exact-size compressed pixel buffer. Bound both the 4-byte marker read
  and the scan-header copy against the remaining input before copying.
  Thanks to quellsec.dev for the report and analysis.
  This fixes DCMTK issue #1238.
  Affects: dcmjpls/libcharls/scan.h

- Fixed heap buffer under-read in RLE decoder:
  DcmRLECodecDecoder::decodeFrame() read *(outputBuffer - 1) to obtain the
  last decoded pixel value for filling the remainder of an incomplete
  stripe. When a stripe decoded to zero bytes, outputBuffer still pointed
  at the base of the allocation, so this access read one byte before the
  buffer. Guard the read with the decoded size and use zero as the fill
  value when the stripe produced no data.
  Thanks to quellsec.dev for the report and analysis.
  This fixes DCMTK issue #1237.
  Affects: dcmdata/libsrc/dcrleccd.cc

**** Changes from 2026.06.28 (eichelberg)

- Fixed NetBSD build:
  Fixed NetBSD build when compiling without OpenSSL.
  Affects: dcmqrdb/tests/tqrlevel.cc

**** Changes from 2026.06.26 (eichelberg)

- Fixed use-after-free error in charset conversion:
  Fixed a use-after-free error that could occur if an instance of class
  DcmSpecificCharacterSet was declared as a global variable. In this case,
  the clean-up code for module oficonv could be executed before the
  destructor of the DcmSpecificCharacterSet instance, causing the latter
  to manipulate a hash table that had already been deleted.
  Thanks to Daniel M. Drucker for the bug report.
  Affects: oficonv/libsrc/citrus_csmapper.c
           oficonv/libsrc/citrus_csmapper.h
           oficonv/libsrc/citrus_mapper.c

**** Changes from 2026.06.24 (onken)

- Fix C4309 truncation warning in QR level test.
  Affects: dcmqrdb/tests/tqrlevel.cc

**** Changes from 2026.06.22 (onken)

- Fixed OOB read in QR index deleteOldestImages():
  The delete loop was bounded only by the bytes still to free
  (DeletedSize < RequiredSize), without checking the array index s against
  the number of collected records nbimages. If the study descriptor's
  StudySize exceeds the combined size of the matching index records (a
  descriptor/index inconsistency), the loop runs past the end of
  StudyArray, reading out of bounds and passing garbage values to
  DB_IdxRead, DB_IdxRemove and unlink().
  Stop the loop once all collected images have been deleted.
  Thanks to Yuxiao Yan for the report.
  This fixes DCMTK issue #1236.
  Affects: dcmqrdb/libsrc/dcmqrdbi.cc

- Fixed stack buffer overflow in QR Level handling:
  DcmQueryRetrieveIndexDatabaseHandle::startFindRequest() and
  startMoveRequest() copied the QueryRetrieveLevel (0008,0052) into an
  uninitialized "char level[50]" via strncpy() with a hard limit. As
  strncpy() does not NUL-terminate when the source is at least as long as
  the limit, level[49] retained stack garbage and the subsequent
  upper-casing loop "for (pc = level; *pc; pc++)" read and wrote past the
  end of the array. The value is attacker-controlled, so the overflow is
  reachable remotely via a C-FIND-RQ or C-MOVE-RQ carrying an oversized
  (0008,0052) element.
  Use OFStandard::strlcpy(), which always NUL-terminates and never
  overflows the destination.
  Thanks to Yuxiao Yan for the report.
  This fixes DCMTK issue #1235.
  Added:   dcmqrdb/tests/Makefile.dep
           dcmqrdb/tests/tqrlevel.cc
  Affects: dcmqrdb/libsrc/dcmqrdbi.cc
           dcmqrdb/tests/CMakeLists.txt
           dcmqrdb/tests/Makefile.in
           dcmqrdb/tests/tests.cc

- Fix narrowing cast in parseAssociate test:
  Cast the strlen() result to unsigned long since otherwise we get
  a VS compiler warning on 64 bit Windows.
  Affects: dcmnet/tests/tparseassoc.cc

- Use size_t for PDU buffer length in DUL helpers:
  readPDU() sizes the receive buffer with a size_t but
  readPDUHead()/readPDUHeadTCP()/readPDUBody()/readPDUBodyTCP() took the
  buffer length as unsigned long.
  Widen the maxLength parameter to size_t throughout so the buffer
  capacity is carried without truncation.  Actually maxLength
  is only actually used to check whether a related buffer has enough space
  to hold a PDU head (6 bytes).  Thus it is no problem to switch from
  unsigned long to size_t in the API.
  Affects: dcmnet/libsrc/dulfsm.cc

- Fixed leak in DUL A-ASSOCIATE PC parse error path:
  When parseAssociate() failed while parsing a Presentation Context item
  of an incoming A-ASSOCIATE-RQ/AC PDU, the error path released the
  partially parsed context with a plain free(context). That freed only the
  struct, leaking the context's transferSyntaxList (created by
  LST_Create()) and every transfer syntax sub-item already enqueued into
  it. As this code runs during association negotiation, before any access
  control, a remote peer could trigger unbounded heap growth by repeatedly
  sending malformed PDUs (memory-exhaustion DoS).
  Deep-free the presentation context on the error path via a new helper
  destroyPresentationContext(), which is now also reused by
  destroyAssociatePDUPresentationContextList(). Added a regression test
  (dcmnet_parseAssociate_presCtx_malformed_transferSyntax) that reproduces
  the leak under tools like LeakSanitizer.
  Thanks to Yuxiao Yan for the report.
  This closes DCMTK issue #1234.
  Affects: dcmnet/include/dcmtk/dcmnet/helpers.h
           dcmnet/libsrc/dulparse.cc
           dcmnet/libsrc/helpers.cc
           dcmnet/tests/tests.cc
           dcmnet/tests/tparseassoc.cc

- Added config(-only) test for "W" dcmqrscp access:
  Added config(-only) test for "W" dcmqrscp access that checks whether
  (at least) the read/write flags are evaluated correctly from the config
  file.
  Added:   dcmqrdb/tests/CMakeLists.txt
           dcmqrdb/tests/taccess.cc
           dcmqrdb/tests/tests.cc
  Affects: dcmqrdb/CMakeLists.txt
           dcmqrdb/Makefile.in
           dcmqrdb/tests/Makefile.in

- Enforce "Access W" for Q/R in dcmqrscp:
  The per-AE "Access" field in the dcmqrscp configuration only gated
  Storage presentation contexts: a non-writable ("R") area correctly had
  C-STORE refused, but there was no corresponding gate for the read paths.
  As a result a "write-only" ("W") storage area still negotiated and served
  C-FIND, C-GET and C-MOVE, disclosing stored metadata and objects from an
  AE the operator intended to be ingest-only.
  Add a readableStorageArea() predicate ("R"/"RW"/"WR" are readable, "W" is
  not) and refuse the Query/Retrieve abstract syntaxes during association
  negotiation for non-readable areas, mirroring the existing storage gate.
  Verification (C-ECHO) and storage (C-STORE) remain available so "W" now
  behaves as documented: ingestion allowed, query/retrieve refused.
  Thanks to Yuxiao Yan for the report.
  This closes DCMTK issue #1233.
  Affects: dcmqrdb/docs/dcmqrcnf.txt
           dcmqrdb/include/dcmtk/dcmqrdb/dcmqrcnf.h
           dcmqrdb/include/dcmtk/dcmqrdb/dcmqrsrv.h
           dcmqrdb/libsrc/dcmqrcnf.cc
           dcmqrdb/libsrc/dcmqrsrv.cc

